=== Batch Error Handling Demo (pycubrid 1.6.1+, issue #186 fix) ===

[setup] Created cookbook_batch_probe (id PRIMARY KEY, name NOT NULL)
[1] Seeded row id=1 (will conflict later)
[2] Submitting batch of 5 statements (stmt #3 duplicates id=1)
[3] Caught IntegrityError as expected: Operation would have caused one or more unique constraint violations. INDEX pk_cookbook_batch_probe_id(B+tree: {{BTREE}}) ON CLASS dba.cookbook_batch_probe(CLASS_OID: {{OID}}). key: 1(OID: {{OID}}). (sqlstate='23000')
    args=('Operation would have caused one or more unique constraint violations. INDEX pk_cookbook_batch_probe_id(B+tree: {{BTREE}}) ON CLASS dba.cookbook_batch_probe(CLASS_OID: {{OID}}). key: 1(OID: {{OID}}).',)
[4] Rows visible in this transaction (uncommitted): 5
    id=1  name=seed
    id=2  name=alpha
    id=3  name=beta
    id=4  name=gamma
    id=5  name=delta

[5] Rolling back the whole batch for all-or-nothing semantics...
[6] Rows after rollback: 1
    id=1  name=seed
    -> only the pre-existing committed row survives; the batch was undone.

--- API summary ---
  cursor.executemany_batch(sql_list, auto_commit=None)
    sql_list:    list[str] of COMPLETE SQL statements
    auto_commit: True = each stmt auto-commits; False = caller commits
  Returns: list[tuple[int, int]] of (result_code, affected_count)
  Raises:  PEP 249 exception on first per-statement failure
  Semantics: valid statements are executed and left uncommitted;
             rollback() for all-or-nothing, then resubmit the batch

See also: pycubrid issue #186 for the silent-swallow bug this fixes.
